#
# `.` takes no operands past the file name in dash, and it is alone in that.
#
# POSIX gives `.` optional operands that become the dot script's positional
# parameters for the length of the file. bash, zsh and ksh93 all pass them;
# dash ignores them, so the dot script sees the caller's `$@` unchanged —
# which means the same script, sourced the same way, reads a different
# argument list here than in any other shell on the panel.
#
# The dot script is written by this file rather than assumed, so what it
# prints is what this run put in it.

printf 'printf "dot sees [%%s][%%s]\\n" "$#" "$1"\n' > d.sh
set -- keep
. ./d.sh extra
printf 'and the caller still has [%s][%s]\n' "$#" "$1"

# With no operand the answer is the caller's list in every shell, which is
# what makes the line above the difference rather than the pair of them.
. ./d.sh

# More operands than the script reads, and an operand that would need
# quoting: neither is passed either.
. ./d.sh one two three
. ./d.sh 'two words'

# `type` on a special builtin says so, where `type echo` does not (#3273).
#
# The distinction is drawn in four of the seven columns and not in the other
# three, so `echo` is the control on every line: a shell that had merely
# adopted a longer phrase would move it too, and a shell that never says
# `special` fails the first line while passing the control. Ours said the
# short sentence in every dialect until #3273, because there was one wording
# and it took only the name.

type .
type eval
type echo

# `command -V` is the same sentence written by another builtin, and it had
# its own road to it.
command -V .
command -V echo

# And *which* names are special is not POSIX's list here: `local` is on the
# special side in this shell and in BusyBox ash, and nowhere else (#3290).
#
# `alias` is the control and is a plain builtin in this column — it is ksh93
# that marks that one — so a shell that had put every name it knows on the
# special side fails the line below rather than passing this file.
type local
type alias
type echo

# The behaviour that comes with the sentence, which is the half a script runs
# into. Inside a function, because `local` outside one ends this shell.
# `command` is the control: an ordinary builtin, whose prefix no column keeps.
f() { LV=1 local x; }
f
printf 'local LV=[%s]\n' "${LV-UNSET}"
g() { CV=1 command true; }
g
printf 'command CV=[%s]\n' "${CV-UNSET}"

# A function may not be named after a special builtin here, and the refusal
# is the parser's: `Syntax error: Bad function name` at 2, before anything on
# the line runs. #2662 recorded this as a two-against-two split with no tier
# to hold it — bash and zsh define the function, ksh93 and dash refuse — and
# measured more closely it is not one. ksh93 refuses **when the definition
# runs**, with `invalid function name` at 1, so `echo before` on the same line
# prints there and not here; bash, zsh and BusyBox ash define it. Three
# answers, and dash is alone in its own, which is what this tier asks
# (measured 2026-09-16).
#
# Through `eval` in a subshell, because a parse refusal in the file itself
# would end the file.
try() { ( eval "$1" ) 2>&1; printf '  -> %s\n' "$?"; }
try 'set() { echo fn; }; echo defined'
try 'export() { echo fn; }; echo defined'
try ':() { echo fn; }; echo defined'
try 'eval() { echo fn; }; echo defined'
try 'return() { echo fn; }; echo defined'
try 'echo before; shift() { echo fn; }'

# The control: `cd` is a regular builtin, so the same line defines a function
# in every column and the function is what runs.
try 'cd() { echo fn; }; cd /; echo defined'
